  "attributions": [
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/1", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::paragraphs_are_read_like_dpkg", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Differential bug on trailing whitespace; the generator's trailing_ws flag feeds the dpkg/python-debian comparisons (now normalised away and gated by skip_for_oracles), and edits_are_read_back disables it."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/2", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::paragraphs_are_read_like_python_debian", "basis": "inferred", "note": "The generator's empty_first_line flag feeds the oracle comparisons, which compare values line by line; python-debian keeps the empty first line the crate drops."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/3", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::paragraphs_are_read_like_dpkg", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Differential bug; the generator's crlf flag feeds the oracle comparisons and nearly every other property forces crlf=false or skips '\\r'."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/4", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::lookups_are_case_insensitive_first_matches", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Gates around it by name (hits_indented_hash_bug) and unwraps the strict parse that this bug makes fail; the oracle parse properties also skip it via skip_for_oracles."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/5", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::incremental_reparse_of_valid_documents_equals_a_full_parse", "basis": "explicit", "note": "Its comment says edits touching blank lines or comments hit deb822-lossless/5 (mentioned_by)."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/6", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::wrap_and_sort_is_canonical", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Gates around it by name (hits_wrap_comment_bug) before wrapping; edits_are_read_back disables wrap edits for the same reason."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/7", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::edits_are_read_back", "basis": "inferred", "note": "The only property exercising remove_paragraph/move_paragraph; skips edits via hits_index_bug."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/8", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::edits_are_read_back", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Skips edits via hits_set_bare_colon_bug, matching the pin's set-after-bare-colon subject."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/9", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::edits_are_read_back", "basis": "inferred", "note": "Skips edits via hits_missing_newline_bug, matching the pin's unterminated-last-line subject."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/10", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::wrap_and_sort_is_canonical", "basis": "explicit", "note": "Its comment says wrap_and_sort on its own output with comments hits deb822-lossless/10 (mentioned_by)."},
    {"bug": "deb822-lossless/11", "property": "rust/deb822-lossless::edits_are_read_back", "basis": "inferred", "note": "The only property applying paragraph moves to multi-paragraph documents; no gate names this bug specifically, so less certain than 7-9."}
  ],
  "batch_notes": "(1) deb822-fast, deb822-lossless and debian-changelog share one author's design: dpkg (Perl, Dpkg::Control::HashCore / dpkg-parsechangelog) and python-debian are driven as persistent child processes as external oracles, and properties gate around known bugs with helper predicates named after the bug's subject (hits_indented_hash_bug, hits_index_bug, hits_plus_bug, ...) rather than citing bug ids, so most attributions here are 'inferred' from gate names; the rubric's 'cites the bug id' criterion for explicit misses this common shape. (2) deb822-fast and debian-changelog record their pins as generative Hegel properties with a fixed skeleton and one random slot (bugs have `property` set, no #[test] pins), whereas deb822-lossless uses deterministic #[test] pins; the same bug-finding is thus counted as a property in two targets and not the third. (3) debian-changelog comments say 'Pin for bug N' even on fully generative properties (closes_lists_agree_with_dpkg draws arbitrary bodies); tagged pin on the doc's word but narrowness varies. (4) Recurring shape the rubric does not name: 'lossless CST' (tree.to_string() == input for any text, with never-panicking accessors) classified as roundtrip; and 'live tree after mutation agrees with a re-parse of its own text' (deb822-lossless/12, debian-changelog/11) classified internal-consistency or spec-postcondition. (5) datafusion's Precision properties compute the expectation from stdlib saturating ops plus a test-written exactness rule; classified model-oracle, borderline external. (6) Quality: data-encoding's README lists every property as '(no doc comment)' although each has a detailed doc comment in the source; the README generator missed them. (7) rust/data-encoding::EncoderMachine has no docs; classified stateful-model per instructions, its driver prop_encoder_stateful_matches_batch_encode as internal-consistency (streaming vs one-shot) with the stateful tag."
}
